From: Cody McGinnis <brainwart@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] interconnect: qcom: sc8280xp: keep the PCI0 BCM alive

Since commit d4c79b63d82d ("PCI: Allow D3 for native hotplug-capable Root
Ports on non-x86 platforms") the hotplug-capable Root Ports on SC8280XP
are put into D3 during system suspend. That makes
pci_host_common_d3cold_possible() true, so qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() takes
the D3cold path and icc_disable()s the "pcie-mem" path of every
controller.

PCI0 (qns_pcie_gem_noc) is only voted by those paths. Once all of them
are disabled, its AMC/WAKE vote drops to zero and RPMh switches the
bridge off while the application processor is still running. The
platform then takes a firmware warm reset during s2idle, before any PCIe
resume_noirq callback runs.

This was confirmed on a ThinkPad X13s with breadcrumbs in PMIC SDAM that
survive the reset. All three controllers completed the D3cold suspend
sequence, and none reached resume. Toggling keepalive on bcm_pci0 at
runtime turned the reset off (5/5 s2idle cycles) and back on (reset on
the first cycle) within the same boot. The CPU-to-PCIe path was not
involved; its BCM, CN0, is already keepalive.

Mark PCI0 keepalive, as CN0, SN0, SH0 and MC0 already are. keepalive only
affects the AMC and WAKE buckets, so the BCM can still be dropped in the
sleep set and SoC low-power states are not affected. Cluster power-down
was still reached on every cycle.

Fixes: d4c79b63d82d ("PCI: Allow D3 for native hotplug-capable Root Ports on non-x86 platforms")
---
--- a/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sc8280xp.c
+++ b/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sc8280xp.c
@@ -2141,6 +2141,7 @@
 
 static struct qcom_icc_bcm bcm_pci0 = {
 	.name = "PCI0",
+	.keepalive = true,
 	.num_nodes = 1,
 	.nodes = { &qns_pcie_gem_noc },
 };
